Here's a focused plan and minimal patch to resolve the heap-buffer-overflow in Mat_VarPrint for MAT_C_SPARSE printing.

What’s going on
- The crash occurs when printing sparse matrices, due to iterating the inner loop with a bound that can read past the allocated data (ndata).
- Current code uses: for ( j = sparse->jc[i]; j < (size_t)sparse->jc[i+1] && j < (size_t)sparse->ndata; j++ )
  and in the complex case it relies on j indexing into re/im arrays as re + j*stride / im + j*stride.
- If sparse->jc[i+1] or sparse->ndata isn’t perfectly bounded, or if there’s off-by-one in data/file, the inner loop can read beyond the end of re/im or data arrays.

Proposed fix
- Use explicit start/end bounds for the inner loop instead of re-reading sparse->jc[i+1] in the loop condition.
- Compute:
  - start = sparse->jc[i]
  - end   = sparse->jc[i+1], clamped to sparse->ndata
  - if start >= end, skip
  - loop: for (j = start; j < end; j++) { ... use j as index into ir, data, Re/Im, etc. }
- Apply the same fix to both the complex and real (non-complex) branches.

Why this fix
- It confines all indexing to the actual end of the data arrays (ndata).
- It eliminates one source of potential out-of-bounds reads caused by using a composite bound expression (jc[i+1] with potentially mismatched ends).

Minimal patch (unified-diff style)
- File: src/matio/src/mat.c
- Change only the inner loops of the MAT_C_SPARSE case, both for complex and non-complex branches.

--- a/src/matio/src/mat.c
+++ b/src/matio/src/mat.c
@@
-                if ( matvar->isComplex ) {
-                    mat_complex_split_t *complex_data = (mat_complex_split_t *)sparse->data;
-                    const char *re = (const char *)complex_data->Re;
-                    const char *im = (const char *)complex_data->Im;
-                    for ( i = 0; i < (size_t)sparse->njc - 1; i++ ) {
-                        for ( j = sparse->jc[i];
-                              j < (size_t)sparse->jc[i + 1] && j < (size_t)sparse->ndata; j++ ) {
-                            printf("    (%u,%" SIZE_T_FMTSTR ")  ", sparse->ir[j] + 1, i + 1);
-                            Mat_PrintNumber(matvar->data_type, re + j * stride);
-                            printf(" + ");
-                            Mat_PrintNumber(matvar->data_type, im + j * stride);
-                            printf("i\n");
-                        }
-                    }
-                } else {
-                    const char *data = (const char *)sparse->data;
-                    for ( i = 0; i < (size_t)sparse->njc - 1; i++ ) {
-                        for ( j = sparse->jc[i];
-                              j < (size_t)sparse->jc[i + 1] && j < (size_t)sparse->ndata; j++ ) {
-                            printf("    (%u,%" SIZE_T_FMTSTR ")  ", sparse->ir[j] + 1, i + 1);
-                            Mat_PrintNumber(matvar->data_type, data + j * stride);
-                            printf("\n");
-                        }
-                    }
-                }
+                if ( matvar->isComplex ) {
+                    mat_complex_split_t *complex_data = (mat_complex_split_t *)sparse->data;
+                    const char *re = (const char *)complex_data->Re;
+                    const char *im = (const char *)complex_data->Im;
+                    for ( i = 0; i + 1 < (size_t)sparse->njc; i++ ) {
+                        size_t start = sparse->jc[i];
+                        size_t end   = sparse->jc[i + 1];
+                        if ( start >= (size_t)sparse->ndata ) continue;
+                        if ( end > (size_t)sparse->ndata ) end = (size_t)sparse->ndata;
+                        if ( start >= end ) continue;
+                        for ( j = (size_t)start; j < (size_t)end; j++ ) {
+                            printf("    (%u,%" SIZE_T_FMTSTR ")  ", sparse->ir[j] + 1, i + 1);
+                            Mat_PrintNumber(matvar->data_type, re + j * stride);
+                            printf(" + ");
+                            Mat_PrintNumber(matvar->data_type, im + j * stride);
+                            printf("i\n");
+                        }
+                    }
+                } else {
+                    const char *data = (const char *)sparse->data;
+                    for ( i = 0; i + 1 < (size_t)sparse->njc; i++ ) {
+                        size_t start = sparse->jc[i];
+                        size_t end   = sparse->jc[i + 1];
+                        if ( start >= (size_t)sparse->ndata ) continue;
+                        if ( end > (size_t)sparse->ndata ) end = (size_t)sparse->ndata;
+                        if ( start >= end ) continue;
+                        for ( j = (size_t)start; j < (size_t)end; j++ ) {
+                            printf("    (%u,%" SIZE_T_FMTSTR ")  ", sparse->ir[j] + 1, i + 1);
+                            Mat_PrintNumber(matvar->data_type, data + j * stride);
+                            printf("\n");
+                        }
+                    }
+                }

Notes
- This patch uses explicit start/end bounds for the inner loop, which prevents reading beyond arrays.
- It preserves the outer loop structure and the existing formatting/printing semantics.
- No other behavior changes are introduced.

What to run for verification
- Build: secb build (in /src/matio)
- Reproduce: secb repro
- Expected: AddressSanitizer heap-buffer-overflow no longer triggers for MAT_VarPrint when printing sparse matrices.

Additional notes
- If there are crafted MAT files with irregular sparse indices, the improved bounds checking will prevent the out-of-bounds reads regardless of jc/i+1 integrity.
- The patch is minimal and targeted to the vulnerable code path; it should not affect non-sparse or non-printing code paths.

